WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.040
Speaker 1: Today we are welcoming author Robert
GREENE to the program, who's written seven

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.620
international bestsellers, including the 48
Laws of Power, The Art of Seduction, The Laws

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.130
of Human Nature, and Others, most of which
I've read.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.340
Robert, really appreciate your time today.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.039
Speaker 4: Thanks for having me.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:19.039
David.

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:20.039
My pleasure.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:23.830
Speaker 1: So one of the things maybe we could
start with is I've read a little bit about

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:24.830
your process.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:30.140
When you write a book and the wealth of material
that you will read and then how you will sort

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:36.110
of catalog and have a repository of the things
you've read in preparation for the books that

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.110
you write.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.000
One of the things that I struggle with personally,
and I know many in our audience do also, is

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.410
how to have a system to determine what is
worth reading, studying or paying attention

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:49.760
to today.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.820
And of course, with social media and endless
streaming content and sports and history and

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.930
economics and all of the different things
that people could spend time reading and learning

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.879
about, what's your process for, even at the
top level, determining what is worthy of our

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.380
attention in 2023?

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:12.799
Speaker 4: Well, I'm very aware of the dangers
of trying to digest too much information and

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:15.189
overloading the brain.

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:19.790
So when I write a book, I try and make it
as focused and concentrated as possible.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:21.340
Excuse me.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.790
I cut out as many distractions as is humanly
possible.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:33.119
And so in the beginning, I choose a subject
that can be rather wide ranging, like Power

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:35.510
or my fifth book, Mastery.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.880
And then I read.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:43.040
I try and find all of the books that deal
with that subject that I think are going to

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.369
be direct and realistic and practical.

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:50.229
My method is I'm trying to really change the
way you look at the world, change the way

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:52.900
you think from the inside out.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:59.719
And the only way to do that is to actually
have a really solid basis in research in reality

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:03.630
so I can make the book as practical as possible.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:10.149
So I sort of start by casting a wide net and
I read all the great books out there on and

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:14.230
excuse me, for instance, on power.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:19.500
And then slowly I start narrowing it down
to things that are a little bit more specific.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.910
And it's kind of a fun process.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:28.430
So if I just kept it too narrow and I just
focused on very small little bits of books

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.519
about power, it wouldn't be fun for me.

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:37.270
Part of the excitement because if I'm excited,
I think the book tends to have be exciting

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:38.270
to the reader.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.940
Part of the excitement is finding things that
I never expected to find.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.560
So I go from one book and then I look at the
bibliography.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.000
It references another book.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.379
It excites me on and on and on.

00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:56.660
By the end of the process, I've read two or
300 books to be able to write one book.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:03.470
And so I'm, you know, I'm reading an awful
lot, but I have to kind of organize the material

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:10.070
and where a lot of books, I think, fail, it's
they they don't they're not organized well.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:15.519
So I've created a system with cards where
I put I read a book and then I put all of

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:20.730
the material on and cards and organize it
by subject by category.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:25.040
And then I could show you, if I ever if you
ever wanted my system of cards.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.640
I have thousands of cards for one book.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.110
So I try and keep my attention very focused
on the things that are incredibly important

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:38.340
and practical to give my book a kind of this
grounding in reality.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.120
And then I organize the material in a very,
almost fetishistic way so that I can make

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.459
it so that the reader doesn't feel like I'm
overloading them with information, even though

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:54.190
I myself have consumed vast amounts of literature
on the subject.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:04:00.540
Speaker 1: Do you have any overarching approach
to, as a consumer of media in which I would

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.519
include books and all of the other forms of
media that are out there?

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:10.939
If people in our audience have at their disposal
books of all sorts of different genres, as

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:16.400
well as audio, visual content, music, all
of the different things playing video games,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.400
etc..

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:24.050
What is an approach to figuring out what is,
quote, worth spending time on versus not worth

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.170
it, which certainly would vary from person
to person?

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:32.139
Is it objective based in terms of what it
is you are looking to achieve over a period

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:37.460
of time and which forms of information consumption
will get you closer?

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.780
Or how should the average person think about
this?

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:45.980
You know, reading your books versus novels,
watching sports, reading narrative nonfiction

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.240
about Teddy Roosevelt or whatever it may be?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.060
What is there an approach that you could suggest?

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.930
Speaker 4: Well, it depends on the individual.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:00.400
And so one thing that I think is really important
is that you have a very firm.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.479
Understanding of who you are and what your
life is about and your objectives in life

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:08.090
and what makes you different from other people.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:15.360
So, so many people don't have that deep connection
to what to what to what their vocation is

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.169
in life, their calling.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:20.910
And so they read anything because they don't
have a filter.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:25.650
So I think it's very, very important and I
talk a lot about this in my book Mastery,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:30.240
as you have a very, very firm sense of this
is where I want to go in life.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.930
This is what connects to me.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.830
This is what excites me.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:38.210
So without that kind of filter, without that
kind of focus, then you're kind of lost at

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:42.139
sea and you're reading all kinds of things
that aren't going to help you in the end.

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:49.139
I mean, at some point I myself like to waste
time, you know, like anybody else.

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:55.150
So I will get on and I'll look at YouTube
videos or podcasts or read material that isn't

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.080
directly related to my work.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:02.810
And I think it's important to sometimes relax
and not be so rigid about this.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:09.389
But I think you have to have a sense of where
of who you are, what you like, what's important

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:15.039
to you, what matters what and where your career
is going.

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:19.000
And then from there you determine what's important
for you to read.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.449
Because the problem we're facing today, quite
frankly, is we're just inundated with too

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:27.720
much information, with trivia that just clogs
the brain up.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.990
It makes it very, very hard for you to focus.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:35.009
It makes it very hard for you to actually
think about your own priorities in life.

00:06:35.009 --> 00:06:39.980
So you can't find an app or some teacher that's
going to help you do that.

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:41.790
It has to come from within.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.039
You have to have a sense of who you are, what
makes you different, what's important to you,

00:06:46.039 --> 00:06:47.039
what your values are.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:52.840
From there you can then filter out the information
is completely useless, or that's more important

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:53.840
for you.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.580
Speaker 1: Do you think that at this point
in time?

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:03.530
It is still the case that there might be some
core curriculum of sorts.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:08.509
The idea of some kind of you know, at one
point it was the classical education where

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:14.150
there were certain works that no matter what
your vocation or interests were, may be worthy

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.270
or almost like a required reading of sorts.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:24.039
Does that idea still ring in any way true
to you today, or has sort of human life on

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:29.160
earth in 2023 become so diverse that that
very idea maybe is no longer relevant?

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:34.220
Speaker 4: Well, when I was going to school,
it was like these classics from I believe

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.500
it was I forget the name of the schools like
Johns Hopkins.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.580
They had a list of all the great classics.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:46.001
I come from a humanities background, and so
there were books that I read that are sort

00:07:46.001 --> 00:07:52.590
of the canon in there that were kind of elemental,
but, you know.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:58.940
So right now we're in a period of time where
science, technology is dominating our schools,

00:07:58.940 --> 00:07:59.940
etc.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.729
Our way of thinking and the humanities have
kind of faded and are actually being kind

00:08:04.729 --> 00:08:05.729
of closed off.

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:09.259
And many universities, people aren't interested
in them anymore.

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:16.199
But I think it's very important to be able
to ground yourself in our history, in our

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:18.590
culture, in our civilization.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:22.900
And some of these classics, I think, are still
extremely relevant and very, very important

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.690
to read.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:30.530
The reason is, is it's not just important
what you read, but it's important how you

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:31.530
read.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:32.530
Right.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:38.080
So my background in classics, because I studied
ancient Greek and Latin, which is probably

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:44.970
the most irrelevant subject you could study,
but I think helped me very, very much my ability

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:49.010
to organize my thoughts and organize material.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:54.070
So a lot of these books, they kind of train
you how to think, how to interpret, how to

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:58.910
read, subtext, how to get inside of a book
and take it apart and see what somebody is

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:00.430
really saying.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:03.670
The ideas behind the ideas, etc., etc..

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:07.830
I find that a lot of that is missing nowadays
in people's thinking.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:11.920
Is getting thinner and thinner and thinner
where they're not really analyzing and not

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.852
going into any kind of depth on the subject
because they're so distracted and they're

00:09:16.852 --> 00:09:21.550
going from subject to subject to subject,
their scroll, etc., etc., etc..

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:26.930
When you read a dense text like I had to read,
like the city's history of the Peloponnesian

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.640
War, it makes you focus on just one paragraph.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.950
What is he trying to say?

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.510
What's the meaning behind it?

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.490
It gives your brain focus and discipline.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:37.490
Right.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:43.640
So I think reading, going back to some of
that is extremely important even here in the

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:44.640
21st century.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.640
And I don't regret at all studying perhaps
the, as I said, the most irrelevant subject

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.160
of all, because I think it gave me a discipline
of mind that I would not have had elsewhere.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.260
But that said, I think it's important to have
a wide ranging interest.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.519
I like to read a lot about science, for instance.

00:10:02.519 --> 00:10:06.720
I don't have a science background, but in
reading a lot of that for the books that I'm

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:12.279
currently writing and it kind of opens my
mind up to different ways of thinking.

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:18.079
It kind of it has a different logic to it
than literature or biography or history has.

00:10:18.079 --> 00:10:23.019
Every kind of subject has its own logic, its
own way of thinking, its own way of exploring

00:10:23.019 --> 00:10:24.190
reality.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:28.839
And the more you open yourself up to these
various different ways of looking at the world,

00:10:28.839 --> 00:10:33.730
you enrich your mind, but you also not just
in the content, but in how you think.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:39.029
So bringing a kind of scientific mentality
to my material has been very important for

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:41.230
my thought processes.

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:46.760
So I would I would throw a wide net out there
and read in many different disciplines, but

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:53.610
kind of ground yourself in a way of analyzing
information instead of just digesting information.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:57.350
Having being able to be critical and analyzing,
I think it's very important.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:05.149
Speaker 1: Along those lines in the books
that you write, there is such a broad range

00:11:05.149 --> 00:11:11.100
of both sorts of folks that you write about
and subject matter that you've read about

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:18.320
and you draw connections and parallels between
different eras in history as well as between

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.290
different disciplines.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:27.430
In all of that, is there anything that seems
particularly different about the current time

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:32.790
that we're living through that seems to be
an outlier based on the patterns and connections

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:36.690
that you've studied so deeply in preparing
all of the books that you've written.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:41.220
Speaker 4: When you consumed so much material
and history, it kind of changes how you think.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.480
And so a lot of people nowadays who don't
read a lot of history, quite frankly, have

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.690
this idea that everything that's going on
now is new and different and modern.

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.230
It's never happened before.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:56.010
There's the old cliche in.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.130
Financial bubbles that this time it's different.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:06.130
You know, like in 2008 with the new way of
doing real estate equity, etc. this time it's

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:07.130
different.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:09.280
You know, with I, this time it's different.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.280
It's never different.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:15.190
It's the same patterns repeating over and
over and over again throughout history.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:20.000
I'm reading a book right now about 12th Century
France.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.560
I'm writing about a couple of characters in
that period.

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.519
It is incredibly modern.

00:12:25.519 --> 00:12:31.139
The ideas, the ways people are thinking about
love, about philosophy, about ancient history.

00:12:31.139 --> 00:12:35.500
There's like characters that I see right now
in here in Los Angeles.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:40.949
There's hipsters, there's guys with long hair,
there's people discussing love and freedom,

00:12:40.949 --> 00:12:41.949
etc.

00:12:41.949 --> 00:12:42.949
It's like they're like hippies.

00:12:42.949 --> 00:12:43.949
Wow.

00:12:43.949 --> 00:12:47.440
This is this is incredibly similar to our
times.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.070
We have this illusion that things are different.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:54.180
The only thing that's different is things
get more intense.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:59.320
So what social media has done is it's taken
qualities that are inherent in human nature

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:07.100
that have existed for 5000, 10,000, 400,000
years, like our propensity for envy, our need

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:14.250
to constantly compare ourselves, like our
self-absorption, like our aggressive impulses,

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.190
and it simply gives them a way to accentuate
them.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:20.100
It gives them like a megaphone, for instance.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:27.670
So our kind of passive aggressive tendencies
or our hostility on the Internet and social

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:30.830
media, it's like it's just exacerbated.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.709
So it's not like things are just like we rewired
ourselves.

00:13:33.709 --> 00:13:37.410
It's not like we've become different people.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.360
Social media is having an effect on our brains.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.850
It's changing certain things.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:42.660
It's making things worse.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:47.200
It's taking qualities that we already have
and making them more and more extreme.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:52.000
But we're the same animal that existed in
ancient Greece, in ancient Egypt, etc.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.390
It's just that we have tools now that we're
not really capable of handling in a in a in

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:00.589
a rational way.

00:14:00.589 --> 00:14:04.690
Our minds were developed for a certain way
of life.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.980
That's completely different in the 21st century.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:13.459
So we have these tools that are so powerful,
but our minds are really adapted, aren't really

00:14:13.459 --> 00:14:17.770
made for it being able to handle them in a
very realistic way.

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:22.740
But I don't think the more I read about history,
the more I have the sense that human human

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:27.910
life, civilizations and cultures go in these
cycles that just keep repeating over and over

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:28.910
and over again.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.630
Speaker 1: So along those lines, since you
brought up social media, a couple of different

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:38.550
things in his two books, Amusing Ourselves
to Death and Technology, which predated Social

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:44.620
media, Neil Postman has an analysis which,
if applied to social media or a critique,

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:51.600
would be the question of whether social media
is a net good is a less important question.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:56.400
Then how can we maximize the good and try
to limit the bad?

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:02.680
Cal Newport, on his podcast not long ago had
a piece about the effect of what he calls

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:08.730
unrestricted social media use for young kids,
particularly girls, but both boys and girls.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:14.810
And he asserted that if the early data ends
up being what it appears to be in something

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:19.670
like ten years, the idea of giving kids unrestricted
access to social media would be sort of like

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:23.880
giving kids cigarets that it would be like,
Wow, how did we ever not realize that that

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:25.660
was a really bad idea?

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:31.069
So sort of a two part question, How interesting
do you think the question is of is social

00:15:31.069 --> 00:15:32.790
media a net good?

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:39.819
And to what is your sense of how the relationship
to social media might change as the as it

00:15:39.819 --> 00:15:42.210
matures and we know more about its impacts?

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:48.709
Speaker 4: Well, you know, things that technology
and things that we create aren't necessarily

00:15:48.709 --> 00:15:50.449
a net good or a net evil.

00:15:50.449 --> 00:15:54.930
They're just simply instruments that we can
use that are at our disposal that can help

00:15:54.930 --> 00:16:01.279
us, you know, So I remember in the early days
of the Internet as a writer, the incredible

00:16:01.279 --> 00:16:08.529
power that it gave me for researching, giving
me access to documents, to academic things,

00:16:08.529 --> 00:16:13.250
to books that I could normally never get or
find, it was amazing.

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:20.550
So it can be an extremely valuable tool for
gathering information, for finding things

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.110
that you have a simple question about how
to fix something in your house.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:26.360
3 seconds later you have it.

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:31.769
It's also an amazing tool for connection,
for communication, or I can communicate to

00:16:31.769 --> 00:16:35.930
people who have similar interest like I do
around the world.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:37.101
It can be fast food.

00:16:37.101 --> 00:16:41.040
It could be a wonderful tool, but it isn't
like that.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:45.990
It's turned into something rather dark and
rather ugly, and it's kind of decaying our

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.410
minds, like too much sugar wood on our teeth.

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:54.120
And the reason is, and I said it in my book,
The Laws of Human Nature is that this is what

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:56.069
human nature is.

00:16:56.069 --> 00:17:01.639
Create something and we slowly, slowly kind
of degrades more, more and more down to the

00:17:01.639 --> 00:17:03.699
lowest common denominator.

00:17:03.699 --> 00:17:05.880
It happened with television.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:07.050
People are going, Whoa!

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:08.050
Television.

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:09.520
We're going to be able to use it now to educate.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:12.819
Our children are going to be educated on this
incredibly new level.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:15.640
People were that naive in the forties and
fifties.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:19.419
And then slowly we go, No, it's not like that
at all.

00:17:19.419 --> 00:17:23.890
So it's not the instrument itself that's potentially
good or bad.

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:24.890
It's our minds.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:30.370
It's how we use them, how we bring human nature,
how we have no discipline, how we just simply

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:33.299
use things without thinking about them.

00:17:33.299 --> 00:17:38.250
So the other thing that happens is something
like the Internet becomes a tool for making

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:43.500
money instead of for information, for connecting,
for communicating.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:44.600
And how do we control that?

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:48.730
We can't really control that because we live
in a capitalistic society, and that's the

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:51.000
nature of these things.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:55.669
But we create these tools and we don't ever
think about how they're going to be applied

00:17:55.669 --> 00:17:58.430
or the negative consequences for them.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.330
Until it's almost too late.

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:05.050
And so when I read now about A.I. and all
the people throwing their arms up about, oh,

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:10.179
how dangerous it is, even Sam Altman, who's
the person developing this, is warning us

00:18:10.179 --> 00:18:11.490
about the dangers of it.

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:15.570
I think it's rather ridiculous and absurd
for these people to be doing that.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:17.240
They're the ones creating it.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:19.450
They're the ones thrusting it upon us.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.840
Why aren't they thinking about that before
they create it?

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:29.130
Why isn't someone like Steve Jobs imagining
the incredible deleterious effects that an

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:30.860
iPhone could have on our brains?

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:33.000
It's like it to me.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.330
It all comes back to human nature.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:39.900
If we think about the fact that we are very
short sighted, that we tend to be locked in

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:46.049
the present, we don't have the instinct of
thinking ahead and going, what are the consequences

00:18:46.049 --> 00:18:48.880
for this particular action, for this particular
technology?

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:54.290
We're going to be following again and again
and again and again into this trap.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:19:00.500
So I think one thing that could happen as
far as the second part of your question is

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:07.620
that there is something in us that is that
is very real, that they cannot endure so much

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.220
of this virtuality in our lives.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.460
We know that we're ruining our brains.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:15.530
We know that it's making it distract, distracting
us to death.

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:23.130
To quote Neil Postman, that it's having a
net negative effect on our ability to focus.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:28.760
And my hope is that people who are younger,
who are in their early twenties, Gen Z, etc.,

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:30.659
they start rebelling against this.

00:19:30.659 --> 00:19:35.799
Like when I my age, I was rebelling against
the world of my parents and sort of the rigid

00:19:35.799 --> 00:19:37.559
and conventionality of it.

00:19:37.559 --> 00:19:40.300
The people go, We want something more real.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:46.490
We don't like this relationship, this, that
it's creating of of so that we're less and

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.080
less social and we're more and more virtual.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:53.760
And there's a real rebellion and people realize
this is what matters.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:55.970
These are our priorities.

00:19:55.970 --> 00:20:00.799
But until we have a sense of what our values
are, of what's important, and to me what is

00:20:00.799 --> 00:20:05.940
important is that we're a social animal and
that we're able to interact with people on

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:11.720
a deep and a profound level, that we're human,
that we're animal, that creates and builds

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:12.720
things.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:15.240
In order to build things, you have to master
a subject.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:17.220
You have to understand it deeply.

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.180
You have to be able to be patient and disciplined.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:28.360
These are things that I find as high art mega
values, better values that we must have discipline,

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:32.679
mastery, social interaction, creativity.

00:20:32.679 --> 00:20:38.610
And if you have that kind of scale of values,
then when you create something new and you

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:44.320
create something like social media and you
go, Well, how does that help us in these things?

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:47.300
Is this going to have a negative effect?

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:51.800
But if you live in a time where there are
no more values, whereas kind of nihilism,

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:55.549
where there's no sort of there's nothing kind
of grounding us of what's matter, what matters

00:20:55.549 --> 00:20:57.920
or nothing will ever change.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:00.760
We'll never get a grasp of how to use these
things properly.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:05.770
Speaker 1: We've been speaking with author
Robert GREENE will be linking to a number

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:07.100
of his books.

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:08.760
Robert, really appreciate your time.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:11.809
I know you're busy and your time and insights
are very much appreciated.

00:21:11.809 --> 00:21:12.809
Speaker 4: Thank you, Dave.

00:21:12.809 --> 00:21:14.109
Thank you very much for having me.

